+orderLib.ORDER_ext_ss : simpExtLib.ssfrag
+orderLib.ORDER_ext_ss_budgeted :
+  searchBudget.budget -> simpExtLib.ssfrag
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Provides the explicitly tracked order-decision fragment.
+
+The extension fragments lift the same ordinary reducer closures used by
+`ORDER_ss` and `ORDER_ss_budgeted`, retaining their context collection
+and budget ownership. Select this fragment before installing it in an
+extension simpset. Ordinary standalone order fragments and their clients
+remain ordinary; there is no simpset promotion operation.
+
+### Example
+
+``` hol4
+val order_ss = simpExtLib.++
+  (trackedSimpDefaults.initial_seed (), orderLib.ORDER_ext_ss);
+```
+
+### See also
+
+[`linarithLib.LINARITH_ext_ss`](#linarithLib.LINARITH_ext_ss)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8180430
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,118 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
+## `GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC :
+  simpExtLib.simp_mode -> simpExtLib.xsimptac_config ->
+  simpExtLib.simpset -> thm list -> tactic
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Globally simplifies a goal under extended fixpoint control.
+
+The configuration type is
+
+``` hol4
+type xsimptac_config =
+  {base : simptac_config,
+   concl_in_fixpoint : bool,
+   imp_rebuild : bool,
+   imp_premises : bool}
+```
+
+where `simptac_config` is
+
+``` hol4
+type simptac_config =
+  {strip : bool,
+   elimvars : bool,
+   droptrues : bool,
+   oldestfirst : bool}
+```
+
+`GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC mode cfg ss ths` repeatedly simplifies every
+assumption using all of the other assumptions, `ths`, and `ss`.  This
+reaches a mutual fixpoint, so a rule exposed by a later assumption can
+cause an earlier one to be revisited.  Change counting avoids
+reprocessing a tail already known to be fixed; this scheduling
+optimization does not change the normal result.
+
+The fields of `#base cfg` control processing of each simplified
+assumption.  `strip` applies `STRIP_ALL_THEN` when putting it back;
+`elimvars` uses an eliminable equality as a variable substitution;
+`droptrues` discards an assumption simplified to `T`; and `oldestfirst`
+selects the oldest assumption first rather than the newest.
+
+The extra fields control the extent of the fixpoint:
+
+* If `concl_in_fixpoint` is `false`, assumptions first reach their
+  fixpoint and the conclusion is then simplified once.  If it is
+  `true`, the conclusion is simplified after every assumption pass.  A
+  changed or structurally split conclusion restarts the assumption
+  pass on each resulting goal.
+* If `imp_rebuild` is `true`, the tactic also looks for a root rewrite
+  of progressively nested, discharged forms `a ==> w`.  On the first
+  hit it replaces that implication, strips its leading implications
+  back into assumptions, and restarts the whole fixpoint.  This permits
+  implication-left rewrites to rebuild the assumption/conclusion
+  structure.  If the field is `false`, this extra root-rewrite search
+  is omitted.
+* If `imp_premises` is `true`, leading implication premises enter the
+  shared assumption fixpoint before simplification of the conclusion.
+
+The `safe` field of `mode` selects the safe or unsafe final-solver list
+for each tactic-level conclusion simplification, both after assumption
+passes when `concl_in_fixpoint` is enabled and in the final conclusion
+pass.  It does not change rewriting: traversal side conditions continue
+to use unsafe solvers, and loopers continue to run.  Simplification of
+each individual assumption is conversion-only.  Rewrite markers in
+`ths` are processed, and the remaining theorems are available
+throughout both the assumption and conclusion phases.
+
+The existing `global_simp_tac base` is equivalent to
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC
+  {safe = false}
+  {base = base, concl_in_fixpoint = false, imp_rebuild = false,
+   imp_premises = false}
+```
+
+Thus its conclusion remains outside the mutual fixpoint and implication
+rebuilding is disabled by default.
+
+### Failure
+
+The tactic does not fail merely because no simplification is possible,
+but it may diverge.  Exceptions from user conversions, loopers, solvers,
+or ordering functions may propagate.
+
+### Example
+
+The following schematic configuration enables both extended modes while
+using the usual stripping and truth-dropping policy.
+
+``` hol4
+val base_cfg =
+  {strip = true, elimvars = false,
+   droptrues = true, oldestfirst = true}
+
+val extended_global =
+  simpExtLib.GEN_GLOBAL_SIMP_TAC
+    {safe = false}
+    {base = base_cfg,
+     concl_in_fixpoint = true,
+     imp_rebuild = true,
+     imp_premises = false}
+    (trackedSimpDefaults.initial_seed ())
+```
+
+It can then be applied as `extended_global ths`.
+
+### See also
+
+[`bossLib.gs`](#bossLib.gs),
+[`simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC`](#simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC),
+[`simpExtLib.SIMP_RULE`](#simpExtLib.SIMP_RULE),
+[`simpExtLib.clear_rules`](#simpExtLib.clear_rules)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.Split.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.Split.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..52e11a8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.Split.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
+## `Split`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.Split : thm -> thm
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Marks a theorem as a split rule in a simplifier theorem-list argument.
+
+`Split th` is logically equivalent to `th`, but its conclusion is wrapped
+in a marker recognised by the simplifier. While processing a theorem
+list, the simplifier removes the marker and applies
+[`simpExtLib.add_split`](#simpExtLib.add_split) to its extension simpset.
+The marker is simplifier-owned; generic `markerLib` does not construct it.
+The marked theorem is therefore installed as a looper rather than as an
+ordinary rewrite.
+
+The theorem must have a database name and the split-rule shape documented
+for [`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC). Whether it is routed to
+conclusion or assumption splitting is determined structurally: a
+syntactically negated right-hand side denotes an assumption split rule.
+
+`Split` is a per-theorem-list mechanism. It neither changes the original
+simpset nor registers `th` in the persistent `[split]` theorem set.
+Use the lowercase `[split]` attribute with `simpExtLib.split_ss` for
+persistent, ancestry-aware registration, or `add_split` to construct a
+modified simpset explicitly.
+
+The marker is processed by `SIMP_CONV`, `SIMP_PROVE`, simplification
+tactics and the `&&` simpset operation. The actual looper runs only in
+the tactic pipeline, so the marker alone causes no split in `SIMP_CONV`
+or `SIMP_PROVE`. For `&&`, it remains installed in the simpset that the
+operation returns.
+
+### Failure
+
+Constructing `Split th` does not validate the split rule. A simplifier
+operation that consumes the marker fails if `th` is malformed or lacks a
+local or stored database name.
+
+### Example
+
+Given the stored theorem `pick_split`, this static tactic fragment enables
+it only for the displayed simplifier call:
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC (trackedSimpDefaults.initial_seed ())
+  [simpExtLib.Split pick_split]
+```
+
+For example, if
+
+``` hol4
+|- !P. P (pick b x y) <=>
+         (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
+```
+
+is the theorem's conclusion, the tactic can split a matching occurrence
+of `pick` without adding a persistent attribute or changing the base.
+
+### See also
+
+[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC),
+[`simpExtLib.add_split`](#simpExtLib.add_split),
+[`simpExtLib.del_split`](#simpExtLib.del_split),
+[`simpExtLib.split_ss`](#simpExtLib.split_ss),
+[`simpLib.Cong`](#simpLib.Cong)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_looper.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_looper.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..097d0db
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_looper.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
+## `add_looper`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.add_looper :
+  string * (simpExtLib.simpset -> tactic) ->
+  simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Adds a named tactic looper to a simpset.
+
+A call to `add_looper (name, make_tac) ss` returns a new simpset.  The
+function `make_tac` is given the active simpset and must return a tactic.
+The name identifies the looper for updating, deletion, pretty-printing,
+and per-invocation exclusion.
+
+Loopers form the tactic layer of simplification.  On a goal, the
+simplifier first rewrites, then tries the selected final solvers.  If no
+solver closes the goal, it tries the registered loopers in list order,
+using the first one that succeeds.  After a looper succeeds, the whole
+sequence is restarted independently on every subgoal it produced.  A
+looper may also close the goal.
+
+A looper must fail when it is not applicable.  In particular, it should
+not behave like `ALL_TAC`: success without progress causes the restart
+to repeat indefinitely when the simpset is unlimited.  Raising
+`Conv.UNCHANGED` is treated as tactic failure.  A successful application
+should close the goal or make genuine progress.
+
+Looper names are unique within a simpset.  If `name` is new,
+`add_looper` appends it, so older registrations have priority.  If the
+name is already present, its function is replaced in its existing
+position.  Thus updating a looper does not change the order in which
+loopers are tried.
+
+The bound installed by `simpExtLib.limit n` also bounds looper execution.
+There are at most `n` successful looper rounds in one top-level
+simplification-tactic invocation, with one counter shared by all
+subgoals.  Failed looper attempts do not consume the counter.  An
+unlimited simpset has no such protection against a non-progressing
+looper.
+
+In a theorem-list argument, `ExclX ("looper:" ^ name)` suppresses it for
+that simplifier invocation without changing the original simpset.  Use
+`del_looper` for persistent removal from a derived simpset.
+
+Loopers are used by tactic simplifiers based on `GEN_SIMP_TAC`, including
+`SIMP_TAC` and `ASM_SIMP_TAC`.  They are deliberately outside the
+conversion boundary: `SIMP_CONV`, `SIMP_PROVE`, `SIMP_RULE`, and
+`ASM_SIMP_RULE` do not run them.  The rule-based phases of compound
+tactics likewise remain conversion-only.
+
+### Failure
+
+`add_looper` itself never fails and does not run the supplied function.
+A later tactic invocation may fail internally or diverge if the looper
+violates the contract above.
+
+### Example
+
+This simpset splits conjunction goals after ordinary simplification has
+reached a fixed point:
+
+``` hol4
+fun split_conjunctions _ = CONJ_TAC
+
+val conjunction_ss =
+  simpExtLib.empty_ss
+  |> simpExtLib.add_looper ("split conjunctions", split_conjunctions)
+
+(* simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC conjunction_ss [] splits p /\ q into p and q. *)
+
+(* This invocation leaves a conjunction goal unsplit. *)
+(* simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC conjunction_ss
+     [simpExtLib.ExclX "looper:split conjunctions"] *)
+```
+
+### See also
+
+[`simpExtLib.del_looper`](#simpExtLib.del_looper),
+[`simpExtLib.set_looper`](#simpExtLib.set_looper),
+[`simpExtLib.looper_ss`](#simpExtLib.looper_ss),
+[`simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC`](#simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC),
+[`simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV`](#simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_safe_solver.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_safe_solver.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b62bec4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_safe_solver.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"}; open simpExtLib;
+## `add_safe_solver`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.add_safe_solver :
+  TraverseExt.ssolver -> simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Adds a named safe final solver to a simpset.
+
+`add_safe_solver solver ss` appends `solver` to the safe solver list of
+`ss`. Solvers are tried in registration order. If that list already
+contains the solver's name, the existing entry is retained and no second
+active entry is created. The explicit addition is still recorded, so it
+survives a later history rebuild that removes a fragment which supplied
+the earlier entry.
+
+Safe solvers are used to solve the whole residual goal after the rewrite
+phase of `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=true}`. They are not used by
+`GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=false}`, and are never used to discharge conditional
+rewrite side conditions inside the simplifier engine. Engine side
+conditions always use the unsafe list, even in safe tactic mode. See
+`add_unsafe_solver` for the solver context and the complete pipeline.
+
+The safe/unsafe distinction controls solver selection; every returned
+theorem is still checked by the HOL kernel and by the simplifier's
+final-solver wrapper.
+
+### Failure
+
+`add_safe_solver` itself never fails. At use time, a `HOL_ERR` from a
+solver causes the remaining safe solvers to be tried; other exceptions
+propagate.
+
+### See also
+
+[`simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver`](#simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver),
+[`simpExtLib.set_safe_solvers`](#simpExtLib.set_safe_solvers),
+[`simpExtLib.remove_solver`](#simpExtLib.remove_solver),
+[`simpExtLib.mk_tactic_solver`](#simpExtLib.mk_tactic_solver),
+[`simpExtLib.safe_solver_ss`](#simpExtLib.safe_solver_ss)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_split.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_split.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a946f9b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_split.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
+## `add_split`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.add_split :
+  thm -> simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Adds one named split rule to a simpset.
+
+A call `add_split th ss` returns a copy of `ss` with a looper that calls
+[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC) using `th`. The theorem must
+be a named database theorem in the split-rule format documented there.
+The input simpset is unchanged.
+
+If the right side of the rule is syntactically a negation, `add_split`
+installs an assumption looper named
+
+``` hol4
+split_asm thy$theorem
+```
+
+Otherwise it installs a conclusion looper named
+
+``` hol4
+split thy$theorem
+```
+
+Adding another looper with the same strategy name replaces the existing
+one in its current position. The rule is used by simplification tactics,
+whose looper pipeline repeatedly simplifies after successful splits. It
+is not run by conversion-only interfaces such as `SIMP_CONV`.
+
+Unlike the `[split]` attribute, `add_split` does not modify the persistent
+split theorem set; its effect is confined to the returned simpset.
+
+### Failure
+
+Fails if `th` is not a well-formed split rule or has no local or stored
+database name. Merely having no matching redex in a later goal is not an
+error in `add_split` itself.
+
+### Example
+
+Given a stored theorem named `pickTheory$pick_split`:
+
+``` hol4
+Theorem pick_split:
+  !P. P (pick b x y) <=>
+      (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
+Proof
+  ...
+QED
+
+val pick_ss = simpExtLib.add_split pick_split
+  (trackedSimpDefaults.initial_seed ());
+
+(* simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC pick_ss [] can now split occurrences of pick. *)
+```
+
+### See also
+
+[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC),
+[`simpExtLib.del_split`](#simpExtLib.del_split),
+[`simpExtLib.split_ss`](#simpExtLib.split_ss),
+[`simpExtLib.Split`](#simpExtLib.Split),
+[`simpExtLib.add_looper`](#simpExtLib.add_looper)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..907cf43
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,118 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"}; open simpExtLib;
+## `add_unsafe_solver`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver :
+  TraverseExt.ssolver -> simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Adds a named unsafe solver to a simpset.
+
+A value of type `TraverseExt.ssolver` has the form
+
+``` hol4
+{name : string,
+ solve : TraverseExt.simp_prover_ctxt -> term -> thm}
+```
+
+Its `solve` function must prove the supplied Boolean term. The prover
+context passed to it has fields
+
+``` hol4
+{stack : term list, context_thms : thm list, recurse : term -> thm}
+```
+
+Here `stack` is the current conditional-rewrite side-condition stack,
+`context_thms` contains the supplied and congruence-derived context
+available at this point, and `recurse c` recursively simplifies `c`,
+returning an equality theorem. A solver may ignore fields it does not
+need.
+
+`add_unsafe_solver solver ss` appends `solver` to the unsafe solver list
+of `ss`. Solvers are tried in registration order. Names are keys: if the
+unsafe list already contains a solver with the same name, the existing
+solver is retained and no second active entry is created. The explicit
+addition is still recorded in the simpset history. Thus it survives a
+later history rebuild that removes a fragment which supplied the earlier
+entry. The safe and unsafe lists are independent, so the same name can
+occur once in each.
+
+Unsafe solvers have two distinct roles.
+
+* **Simplifier-engine side conditions.** When a conditional rewrite or
+  reducer produces a condition `c`, the engine first recursively
+  simplifies it, or applies the simpset's configured subgoaler. With a
+  solver pipeline active, failed recursive simplification is treated as
+  the reflexive equality on `c`, allowing the solvers to try the original
+  condition. If preprocessing produces `|- c = T`, the condition is
+  solved immediately. Otherwise,
+  each unsafe solver is asked to prove the residual right-hand side; a
+  proof of `c'` is transported back through `|- c = c'`. Safe solvers
+  are never used at this engine seam. This remains true when
+  `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=true}` is used: recursive rewrite side conditions
+  always use the unsafe list.
+
+* **Tactic-layer final solving.** After the rewrite phase of
+  `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=false}`, the unsafe solvers are tried on the whole
+  residual goal, before any looper. For `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=true}`, only
+  the safe list is used for this final step. Thus `SIMP_TAC`,
+  `ASM_SIMP_TAC`, and their usual unsafe-mode descendants use unsafe
+  solvers both inside rewriting and as final solvers. `SIMP_CONV` has no
+  tactic-layer final step, but does use unsafe solvers for rewrite side
+  conditions.
+
+For a final-solver call, `stack` is empty, `context_thms` contains the
+available tactic assumptions and supplied context theorems, and
+`recurse` simplifies with the invocation simpset and that context.
+
+At the engine seam, a `HOL_ERR` raised by one solver means that the next
+solver is tried; other exceptions propagate. A successful solver must
+return a theorem whose conclusion is the requested term. Final solving
+also rejects a theorem with the wrong conclusion or with hypotheses not
+accounted for by the tactic goal. Consequently, “unsafe” describes when
+the solver is selected, not a relaxation of HOL's kernel checking.
+
+A named solver can be suppressed for one simplifier invocation with
+`ExclX ("solver:" ^ name)`, or removed with `remove_solver`.
+Ordinary `Excl` only excludes literal rewrite/conversion/reducer names.
+
+### Failure
+
+`add_unsafe_solver` itself never fails. A registered solver may fail or
+raise an exception when it is later invoked, as described above.
+
+### Example
+
+The following solver proves a condition when that condition is already
+present among the simplifier's context theorems.
+
+``` hol4
+fun context_solver
+      ({context_thms,...} : TraverseExt.simp_prover_ctxt)
+      condition =
+  case List.find
+         (fn th => aconv (concl th) condition) context_thms of
+      SOME th => th
+    | NONE =>
+        raise mk_HOL_ERR "example" "context_solver" "not applicable";
+
+val ss =
+  simpExtLib.add_unsafe_solver
+    {name = "context lookup", solve = context_solver}
+    (trackedSimpDefaults.initial_seed ());
+```
+
+For tactic-based solvers, `mk_tactic_solver` is usually more convenient.
+
+### See also
+
+[`simpExtLib.add_safe_solver`](#simpExtLib.add_safe_solver),
+[`simpExtLib.set_unsafe_solvers`](#simpExtLib.set_unsafe_solvers),
+[`simpExtLib.remove_solver`](#simpExtLib.remove_solver),
+[`simpExtLib.set_subgoaler`](#simpExtLib.set_subgoaler),
+[`simpExtLib.mk_tactic_solver`](#simpExtLib.mk_tactic_solver),
+[`simpExtLib.solver_ss`](#simpExtLib.solver_ss),
+[`simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV`](#simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV),
+[`simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC`](#simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.clear_rules.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.clear_rules.smd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d0e0cdc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/help/Docfiles/simpExtLib.clear_rules.smd
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
+## `clear_rules`
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.clear_rules : simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
+```
+
+------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Removes simplification rules while retaining a simpset's strategy.
+
+A call to `clear_rules ss` returns a simpset with all of the following
+removed:
+
+* ordinary rewrite rules and simpset conversions;
+* congruence rules, relation simplification data, and decision
+  procedures;
+* loopers, including splitters installed as loopers; and
+* the added-fragment history and its fragment names.
+
+The result retains all of the following:
+
+* the rewrite-maker function, including filters used when later
+  theorems are added;
+* the simpset limit and the set of excluded fragment names;
+* the unsafe and safe final-solver lists and the subgoaler;
+* the configured conditional depth and term ordering; and
+* recorded per-invocation looper exclusions.
+
+The retained settings are recorded in the new history, so they survive
+later history-rebuilding operations such as removing a fragment added
+after the call.  The basic equality traversal remains available.
+
+Consequently,
+
+``` hol4
+simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC (simpExtLib.clear_rules ss) ths
+```
+
+is the rewrite-rule analogue of “simplify only with `ths`”.  It is not
+a claim that no other tactic mechanism can act: retained subgoaling and
+final solvers may still prove conditions or goals, and the retained
+rewrite maker controls how `ths` become rewrite rules.  Loopers and old
+decision procedures, however, cannot act because they were cleared.
+
+### Failure
+
+Never fails.
+
+### Example
+
+This schematic tactic ignores the rules and fragments in a tracked base, but
+uses its rewrite-maker and strategy settings when adding the supplied
+rules.
+
+``` hol4
+val only_ss = simpExtLib.clear_rules (trackedSimpDefaults.initial_seed ())
+val only_tac = simpExtLib.SIMP_TAC only_ss [rule1, rule2]
+```
+
+### See also
+
+[`simpExtLib.mk_simpset`](#simpExtLib.mk_simpset),
+[`simpExtLib.remove_ssfrags`](#simpExtLib.remove_ssfrags),
+[`simpExtLib.exclude_ssfrags`](#simpExtLib.exclude_ssfrags),
