diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.add_split.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.add_split.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index 8dbdfa8..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.add_split.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,67 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `add_split`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.add_split : thm -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Adds one named split rule to a simpset.
-
-A call `add_split th ss` returns a copy of `ss` with a looper that calls
-[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC) using `th`. The theorem must
-be a named database theorem in the split-rule format documented there.
-The input simpset is unchanged.
-
-If the right side of the rule is syntactically a negation, `add_split`
-installs an assumption looper named
-
-``` hol4
-split_asm thy$theorem
-```
-
-Otherwise it installs a conclusion looper named
-
-``` hol4
-split thy$theorem
-```
-
-Adding another looper with the same strategy name replaces the existing
-one in its current position. The rule is used by simplification tactics,
-whose looper pipeline repeatedly simplifies after successful splits. It
-is not run by conversion-only interfaces such as `SIMP_CONV`.
-
-Unlike the `[split]` attribute, `add_split` does not modify the persistent
-split theorem set; its effect is confined to the returned simpset.
-
-### Failure
-
-Fails if `th` is not a well-formed split rule or has no local or stored
-database name. Merely having no matching redex in a later goal is not an
-error in `add_split` itself.
-
-### Example
-
-Given a stored theorem named `pickTheory$pick_split`:
-
-``` hol4
-Theorem pick_split:
-  !P. P (pick b x y) <=>
-      (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
-Proof
-  ...
-QED
-
-val pick_ss = simpLib.add_split pick_split bool_ss;
-
-(* SIMP_TAC pick_ss [] can now split occurrences of pick. *)
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC),
-[`simpLib.del_split`](#simpLib.del_split),
-[`simpLib.split_ss`](#simpLib.split_ss),
-[`simpLib.Split`](#simpLib.Split),
-[`simpLib.add_looper`](#simpLib.add_looper)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.add_unsafe_solver.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.add_unsafe_solver.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index 06a0c5f..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.add_unsafe_solver.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,116 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"}; open simpLib;
-## `add_unsafe_solver`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.add_unsafe_solver : Traverse.ssolver -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Adds a named unsafe solver to a simpset.
-
-A value of type `Traverse.ssolver` has the form
-
-``` hol4
-{name : string,
- solve : Traverse.simp_prover_ctxt -> term -> thm}
-```
-
-Its `solve` function must prove the supplied Boolean term. The prover
-context passed to it has fields
-
-``` hol4
-{stack : term list, context_thms : thm list, recurse : term -> thm}
-```
-
-Here `stack` is the current conditional-rewrite side-condition stack,
-`context_thms` contains the supplied and congruence-derived context
-available at this point, and `recurse c` recursively simplifies `c`,
-returning an equality theorem. A solver may ignore fields it does not
-need.
-
-`add_unsafe_solver solver ss` appends `solver` to the unsafe solver list
-of `ss`. Solvers are tried in registration order. Names are keys: if the
-unsafe list already contains a solver with the same name, the existing
-solver is retained and no second active entry is created. The explicit
-addition is still recorded in the simpset history. Thus it survives a
-later history rebuild that removes a fragment which supplied the earlier
-entry. The safe and unsafe lists are independent, so the same name can
-occur once in each.
-
-Unsafe solvers have two distinct roles.
-
-* **Simplifier-engine side conditions.** When a conditional rewrite or
-  reducer produces a condition `c`, the engine first recursively
-  simplifies it, or applies the simpset's configured subgoaler. With a
-  solver pipeline active, failed recursive simplification is treated as
-  the reflexive equality on `c`, allowing the solvers to try the original
-  condition. If preprocessing produces `|- c = T`, the condition is
-  solved immediately. Otherwise,
-  each unsafe solver is asked to prove the residual right-hand side; a
-  proof of `c'` is transported back through `|- c = c'`. Safe solvers
-  are never used at this engine seam. This remains true when
-  `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=true}` is used: recursive rewrite side conditions
-  always use the unsafe list.
-
-* **Tactic-layer final solving.** After the rewrite phase of
-  `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=false}`, the unsafe solvers are tried on the whole
-  residual goal, before any looper. For `GEN_SIMP_TAC {safe=true}`, only
-  the safe list is used for this final step. Thus `SIMP_TAC`,
-  `ASM_SIMP_TAC`, and their usual unsafe-mode descendants use unsafe
-  solvers both inside rewriting and as final solvers. `SIMP_CONV` has no
-  tactic-layer final step, but does use unsafe solvers for rewrite side
-  conditions.
-
-For a final-solver call, `stack` is empty, `context_thms` contains the
-available tactic assumptions and supplied context theorems, and
-`recurse` simplifies with the invocation simpset and that context.
-
-At the engine seam, a `HOL_ERR` raised by one solver means that the next
-solver is tried; other exceptions propagate. A successful solver must
-return a theorem whose conclusion is the requested term. Final solving
-also rejects a theorem with the wrong conclusion or with hypotheses not
-accounted for by the tactic goal. Consequently, “unsafe” describes when
-the solver is selected, not a relaxation of HOL's kernel checking.
-
-A named solver can be suppressed for one simplifier invocation with
-`Excl name`, and can be removed persistently with `remove_solver`.
-
-### Failure
-
-`add_unsafe_solver` itself never fails. A registered solver may fail or
-raise an exception when it is later invoked, as described above.
-
-### Example
-
-The following solver proves a condition when that condition is already
-present among the simplifier's context theorems.
-
-``` hol4
-fun context_solver
-      ({context_thms,...} : Traverse.simp_prover_ctxt)
-      condition =
-  case List.find
-         (fn th => aconv (concl th) condition) context_thms of
-      SOME th => th
-    | NONE =>
-        raise mk_HOL_ERR "example" "context_solver" "not applicable";
-
-val ss =
-  simpLib.add_unsafe_solver
-    {name = "context lookup", solve = context_solver}
-    boolSimps.bool_ss;
-```
-
-For tactic-based solvers, `mk_tactic_solver` is usually more convenient.
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_safe_solver`](#simpLib.add_safe_solver),
-[`simpLib.set_unsafe_solvers`](#simpLib.set_unsafe_solvers),
-[`simpLib.remove_solver`](#simpLib.remove_solver),
-[`simpLib.set_subgoaler`](#simpLib.set_subgoaler),
-[`simpLib.mk_tactic_solver`](#simpLib.mk_tactic_solver),
-[`simpLib.solver_ss`](#simpLib.solver_ss),
-[`simpLib.SIMP_CONV`](#simpLib.SIMP_CONV),
-[`simpLib.SIMP_TAC`](#simpLib.SIMP_TAC)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.clear_rules.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.clear_rules.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index 1ccda68..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.clear_rules.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,68 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `clear_rules`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.clear_rules : simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Removes simplification rules while retaining a simpset's strategy.
-
-A call to `clear_rules ss` returns a simpset with all of the following
-removed:
-
-* ordinary rewrite rules and simpset conversions;
-* congruence rules, relation simplification data, and decision
-  procedures;
-* loopers, including splitters installed as loopers; and
-* the added-fragment history and its fragment names.
-
-The result retains all of the following:
-
-* the rewrite-maker function, including filters used when later
-  theorems are added;
-* the simpset limit and the set of excluded fragment names;
-* the unsafe and safe final-solver lists and the subgoaler;
-* the configured conditional depth and term ordering; and
-* recorded per-invocation looper exclusions.
-
-The retained settings are recorded in the new history, so they survive
-later history-rebuilding operations such as removing a fragment added
-after the call.  The basic equality traversal remains available.
-
-Consequently,
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.SIMP_TAC (simpLib.clear_rules ss) ths
-```
-
-is the rewrite-rule analogue of “simplify only with `ths`”.  It is not
-a claim that no other tactic mechanism can act: retained subgoaling and
-final solvers may still prove conditions or goals, and the retained
-rewrite maker controls how `ths` become rewrite rules.  Loopers and old
-decision procedures, however, cannot act because they were cleared.
-
-### Failure
-
-Never fails.
-
-### Example
-
-This schematic tactic ignores the rules and fragments in `bool_ss`, but
-uses its rewrite-maker and strategy settings when adding the supplied
-rules.
-
-``` hol4
-val only_ss = simpLib.clear_rules bool_ss
-val only_tac = simpLib.SIMP_TAC only_ss [rule1, rule2]
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.mk_simpset`](#simpLib.mk_simpset),
-[`simpLib.remove_ssfrags`](#simpLib.remove_ssfrags),
-[`simpLib.exclude_ssfrags`](#simpLib.exclude_ssfrags),
-[`simpLib.SIMP_TAC`](#simpLib.SIMP_TAC),
-[`simpLib.set_cond_depth`](#simpLib.set_cond_depth),
-[`simpLib.set_term_ord`](#simpLib.set_term_ord)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.del_looper.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.del_looper.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index a94c7c5..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.del_looper.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,59 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `del_looper`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.del_looper : string -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Removes a named tactic looper from a simpset.
-
-A call to `del_looper name ss` returns a new simpset without the looper
-whose registration name is exactly `name`.  Other loopers retain their
-relative order.  If a looper with that name is subsequently added again,
-it is new at that point and is appended after the remaining loopers.
-
-The removal is recorded as a simpset strategy update, so it is preserved
-when later operations rebuild the simpset from its fragment history.
-The operation does not remove rewrite rules, solvers, or a simpset
-fragment merely because one of them has the same name.
-
-For a temporary alternative, placing `Excl name` in a simplification
-tactic's theorem list suppresses the matching looper for that invocation
-only.  This is often preferable when the looper should remain available
-in the reusable simpset.
-
-Loopers run only after rewriting and final solving have failed.  The
-first applicable looper is used, and simplification restarts on all of
-its subgoals.  The simpset bound set by `simpLib.limit` caps successful
-looper rounds across the whole tactic invocation.  Removing a looper
-therefore also removes its participation in this ordered, bounded loop.
-
-This operation affects tactic simplification only.  Loopers do not cross
-into `SIMP_CONV`, `SIMP_PROVE`, `SIMP_RULE`, or `ASM_SIMP_RULE`, whether
-or not they are present in the simpset.
-
-### Failure
-
-If no looper has the given name, `del_looper` issues a HOL warning and
-returns the original simpset unchanged.  It does not raise an exception
-for that case.
-
-### Example
-
-``` hol4
-val with_split =
-  simpLib.add_looper
-    ("split conjunctions", fn _ => CONJ_TAC)
-    simpLib.empty_ss
-
-val without_split =
-  simpLib.del_looper "split conjunctions" with_split
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_looper`](#simpLib.add_looper),
-[`simpLib.set_looper`](#simpLib.set_looper),
-[`simpLib.looper_ss`](#simpLib.looper_ss)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.del_split.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.del_split.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index 4260061..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.del_split.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,51 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `del_split`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.del_split : string -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Removes a named split looper from a simpset.
-
-For an unprefixed argument `name`, a call `del_split name ss` looks for
-both `"split " ^ name` and `"split_asm " ^ name` and removes whichever
-are present. The name is normally a fully qualified theorem database
-name such as `pickTheory$pick_split`.
-
-An argument already beginning with `"split "` or `"split_asm "` is
-treated as an exact strategy name, so only that one looper is considered.
-No theory qualification or short-name expansion is otherwise performed.
-
-Only the returned simpset is changed. In particular, `del_split` does
-not remove a theorem registered in the persistent `[split]` set used by
-[`simpLib.split_ss`](#simpLib.split_ss). Such a dynamically selected rule
-can instead be suppressed for a simplifier invocation with an `Excl`
-marker naming its split strategy.
-
-### Failure
-
-Never fails. If no candidate strategy name is present, the original
-simpset is returned without a warning.
-
-### Example
-
-Continuing with a simpset containing the stored conclusion rule
-`pickTheory$pick_split`:
-
-``` hol4
-val without_pick =
-  simpLib.del_split "pickTheory$pick_split" pick_ss;
-
-(* This exact form removes only the conclusion-rule strategy. *)
-val also_without_pick =
-  simpLib.del_split "split pickTheory$pick_split" pick_ss;
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_split`](#simpLib.add_split),
-[`simpLib.split_ss`](#simpLib.split_ss),
-[`simpLib.Split`](#simpLib.Split),
-[`simpLib.del_looper`](#simpLib.del_looper)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.filter_rewrites.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.filter_rewrites.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index d8970eb..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.filter_rewrites.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,50 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `filter_rewrites`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.filter_rewrites :
-  ((simpLib.thname option * thm) -> bool) -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Removes the rewrite rules a predicate rejects from a simpset.
-
-A call to `filter_rewrites keep ss` returns a simpset that is the same
-as `ss` except that every rewrite rule for which `keep` returns false
-is gone.  The predicate sees each rule as it was added: its theorem,
-and the name it was added under where it has one.  Conversions,
-congruences, decision procedures, loopers and the simpset's strategy
-settings are left alone.
-
-The result is built by replaying the simpset's history, so everything
-the simpset carries beyond its fragments — the rules it has excluded by
-name with `remove_simps` or `-*`, its fragment exclusions, its limit,
-its rewrite maker, its strategy events — stands in the result as it
-stood in `ss`.  Rebuilding a filtered simpset out of `ssfrags_of`
-instead does not preserve them: the fragments a simpset was built from
-are not the simpset.
-
-Where `keep` rejects nothing, the simpset itself is returned.
-
-### Failure
-
-Never fails, and never raises `Conv.UNCHANGED`.
-
-### Example
-
-Withholding one particular theorem from an otherwise ambient simpset,
-whatever fragment it arrived in:
-
-``` hol4
-val without_thm =
-  simpLib.filter_rewrites
-    (fn (_, th) => not (aconv (concl th) (concl unwanted))) (srw_ss())
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.remove_ssfrags`](#simpLib.remove_ssfrags),
-[`simpLib.remove_simps`](#simpLib.remove_simps),
-[`simpLib.clear_rules`](#simpLib.clear_rules),
-[`simpLib.mk_simpset`](#simpLib.mk_simpset)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.looper_ss.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.looper_ss.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index b65b3a1..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.looper_ss.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,71 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `looper_ss`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.looper_ss : string * (simpset -> tactic) -> ssfrag
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Packages one named tactic looper as a simpset fragment.
-
-A call to `looper_ss (name, make_tac)` creates an anonymous `ssfrag` that
-contains the looper and no rewrite rules, conversions, congruences,
-decision procedures, or solvers.  It can be merged into a simpset with
-`++`, or combined with other fragments using `merge_ss`.
-
-When a fragment is merged, looper registration follows the same rules as
-`add_looper`.  A new name is appended after existing loopers.  An
-existing name is updated in place, retaining its priority.  Multiple
-loopers in merged fragments are processed in fragment-list order.  At
-run time the first applicable registered looper is selected.
-
-The function `make_tac` is passed the active, per-invocation simpset.  Its
-tactic is tried only after ordinary rewriting and final solvers fail.
-If it succeeds, the complete simplifier sequence is restarted on each
-subgoal.  It must fail when inapplicable and must make progress whenever
-it succeeds; `Conv.UNCHANGED` is interpreted as failure.
-
-The simpset bound installed by `simpLib.limit n` permits at most `n`
-successful looper rounds in one top-level simplification-tactic call.
-The counter is shared by all subgoals.  With no bound, a looper that
-succeeds without progress may diverge.
-
-The looper registration name is independent of the fragment name.  The
-result of `looper_ss` is anonymous; apply `name_ss` if the fragment must
-be removable with `remove_ssfrags` or suppressible with `ExclSF`.
-Regardless of the fragment name, `Excl name` suppresses this particular
-looper for one theorem-list invocation.
-
-Looper payloads are tactic hooks.  They are used by simplification
-tactics based on `GEN_SIMP_TAC`, but ignored by the conversion and rule
-interfaces `SIMP_CONV`, `SIMP_PROVE`, `SIMP_RULE`, and `ASM_SIMP_RULE`.
-
-### Failure
-
-`looper_ss` never fails and does not run the supplied function.  Giving
-the resulting anonymous fragment to an operation that requires a named
-fragment can fail unless it has first been passed through `name_ss`.
-
-### Example
-
-``` hol4
-val CONJ_LOOP_ss =
-  simpLib.looper_ss
-    ("split conjunctions", fn _ => CONJ_TAC)
-  |> simpLib.name_ss "CONJ_LOOP"
-
-val ss = simpLib.empty_ss ++ CONJ_LOOP_ss
-```
-
-Here `Excl "split conjunctions"` disables the tactic while retaining the
-fragment.  `ExclSF "CONJ_LOOP"` removes the named fragment, including
-its looper, for that invocation.
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_looper`](#simpLib.add_looper),
-[`simpLib.del_looper`](#simpLib.del_looper),
-[`simpLib.set_looper`](#simpLib.set_looper),
-[`simpLib.SSFRAG`](#simpLib.SSFRAG),
-[`simpLib.SIMP_CONV`](#simpLib.SIMP_CONV)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.mk_tactic_solver.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.mk_tactic_solver.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index 8c7587e..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.mk_tactic_solver.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,57 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"}; open simpLib;
-## `mk_tactic_solver`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.mk_tactic_solver : string * tactic -> Traverse.ssolver
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Adapts a tactic into a named simplifier solver.
-
-`mk_tactic_solver (name,tac)` returns a solver named `name`. When asked
-to prove a condition `c`, the solver runs `tac` to completion on the
-goal
-
-``` hol4
-(map concl context_thms, c)
-```
-
-where `context_thms` is the theorem context supplied by the simplifier.
-It then uses `PROVE_HYP` with the actual context theorems. This replaces
-the temporary assumptions introduced for `TAC_PROOF` and preserves any
-hypotheses on which those context theorems themselves depend. The
-solver's `stack` and `recurse` context fields are not used by this
-adapter.
-
-The tactic must solve its goal completely. The resulting solver can be
-registered directly with `add_unsafe_solver` or `add_safe_solver`, or
-packaged in a fragment with `solver_ss` or `safe_solver_ss`.
-
-### Failure
-
-Constructing the solver never fails. At use time, a `HOL_ERR` from the
-tactic is treated as solver non-applicability, so the pipeline can try
-its next solver; other exceptions propagate.
-
-### Example
-
-``` hol4
-val assumption_solver =
-  simpLib.mk_tactic_solver
-    ("assumption", FIRST_ASSUM ACCEPT_TAC);
-
-val ss =
-  simpLib.add_unsafe_solver assumption_solver boolSimps.bool_ss;
-```
-
-This solver can close a side condition that one of the simplifier's
-context theorems proves exactly.
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_unsafe_solver`](#simpLib.add_unsafe_solver),
-[`simpLib.add_safe_solver`](#simpLib.add_safe_solver),
-[`simpLib.solver_ss`](#simpLib.solver_ss),
-[`simpLib.safe_solver_ss`](#simpLib.safe_solver_ss),
-[`simpLib.set_subgoaler`](#simpLib.set_subgoaler)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.remove_solver.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.remove_solver.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index cfadf74..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.remove_solver.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,36 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"}; open simpLib;
-## `remove_solver`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.remove_solver : string -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Removes a named solver from a simpset.
-
-`remove_solver name ss` removes every entry called `name` from both the
-safe and unsafe solver lists of `ss`. If neither list contains `name`,
-the simpset is returned unchanged and no warning is issued.
-
-This operation is persistent on the returned simpset. To suppress a
-named solver only for one simplifier invocation, pass `Excl name` in
-that invocation's theorem list.
-
-### Failure
-
-Never fails.
-
-### Example
-
-``` hol4
-val ss_without_arithmetic =
-  simpLib.remove_solver "linear arithmetic" ss;
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_unsafe_solver`](#simpLib.add_unsafe_solver),
-[`simpLib.add_safe_solver`](#simpLib.add_safe_solver),
-[`simpLib.set_unsafe_solvers`](#simpLib.set_unsafe_solvers),
-[`simpLib.set_safe_solvers`](#simpLib.set_safe_solvers)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.safe_solver_ss.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.safe_solver_ss.smd
