diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.set_subgoaler.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.set_subgoaler.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index d09f145..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.set_subgoaler.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,67 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"}; open simpLib;
-## `set_subgoaler`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.set_subgoaler : Traverse.subgoaler -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Sets the procedure that preprocesses simplifier side conditions.
-
-A `Traverse.subgoaler` has type
-
-``` hol4
-Traverse.simp_prover_ctxt -> term -> thm
-```
-
-Given a prover context and a condition `c`, it must return an equality
-of the form `|- c = c'`. The context contains the current
-side-condition stack, the available context theorems, and a `recurse`
-function which recursively simplifies a term under that same context.
-
-When a conditional rewrite requests a proof of `c`, the configured
-subgoaler is called first. If its result is `|- c = T`, the condition is
-solved. Otherwise the simpset's unsafe solvers are tried, in order, on
-`c'`; a proof of `c'` is transported through the equality to prove `c`.
-Thus a subgoaler may normalize a condition into a form suitable for a
-specialized solver, rather than proving the condition outright.
-
-`set_subgoaler subgoaler ss` replaces any subgoaler already configured
-on `ss`. The setting affects side conditions in `SIMP_CONV` and in the
-rewrite phase of simplification tactics. It does not configure a
-separate tactic-layer final solver.
-
-If no subgoaler is configured, recursive simplification itself is used.
-There is currently no public operation for restoring `NONE`; retain the
-original simpset if both configured and unconfigured variants are
-needed.
-
-### Failure
-
-`set_subgoaler` itself never fails. At use time, a malformed equality or
-an exception from the subgoaler causes the surrounding side-condition
-attempt to fail or propagate according to the exception involved.
-
-### Example
-
-A subgoaler that explicitly selects the standard recursive
-simplification is:
-
-``` hol4
-fun recursive_subgoaler
-      ({recurse,...} : Traverse.simp_prover_ctxt) condition =
-  recurse condition;
-
-val ss' = simpLib.set_subgoaler recursive_subgoaler ss;
-```
-
-More specialized subgoalers can inspect `stack` and `context_thms`
-before calling `recurse`.
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.add_unsafe_solver`](#simpLib.add_unsafe_solver),
-[`simpLib.mk_tactic_solver`](#simpLib.mk_tactic_solver),
-[`simpLib.set_cond_depth`](#simpLib.set_cond_depth),
-[`simpLib.SIMP_CONV`](#simpLib.SIMP_CONV)
diff --git a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.set_term_ord.smd b/help/Docfiles/simpLib.set_term_ord.smd
deleted file mode 100644
index 587cd73..0000000
--- a/help/Docfiles/simpLib.set_term_ord.smd
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,58 +0,0 @@
->>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
-## `set_term_ord`
-
-``` hol4
-simpLib.set_term_ord : (term * term -> order) -> simpset -> simpset
-```
-
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-
-Sets the term ordering used for permutative rewrites in a simpset.
-
-A rewrite is permutative when its two sides differ only by a permutation
-of variables, as with commutativity.  For an instantiated permutative
-rewrite with left- and right-hand sides `l` and `r`, the simplifier
-applies the rewrite only when the configured function returns
-`GREATER` on `(l,r)`.  The ordering should be a total, AC-compatible
-term ordering; an unsuitable function can prevent normalization or
-make it unstable.
-
-The ordering is dynamically bound for each simplifier traversal.  The
-previous value of `Cond_rewr.term_ord` is restored on normal return and
-on an exception, and nested invocations restore their caller's value.
-A simpset on which `set_term_ord` has not been called uses
-`!Cond_rewr.term_ord` at invocation time.  That reference is initially
-`Cond_rewr.ac_term_ord`.
-
-The ordering guard is deliberately bypassed for bounded rewrites, such
-as rules marked with `Once` or `Ntimes`: their usage bound prevents the
-unrestricted permutative loop that the ordering normally avoids.
-Non-permutative rewrites are also unaffected by this setting.
-
-### Failure
-
-Never fails.  A permutative rewrite rejected by the ordering is simply
-not applied.  Exceptions raised by the supplied ordering function can
-propagate when the simpset is used.
-
-### Example
-
-This schematic example reverses the standard order and stores the
-resulting policy in a new simpset.
-
-``` hol4
-fun reverse_order pair =
-  case Cond_rewr.ac_term_ord pair of
-      LESS => GREATER
-    | EQUAL => EQUAL
-    | GREATER => LESS
-
-val reverse_ss = simpLib.set_term_ord reverse_order bool_ss
-```
-
-### See also
-
-[`simpLib.set_cond_depth`](#simpLib.set_cond_depth),
-[`BoundedRewrites.Once`](#BoundedRewrites.Once),
-[`BoundedRewrites.Ntimes`](#BoundedRewrites.Ntimes),
-[`simpLib.clear_rules`](#simpLib.clear_rules)
